import RevPebbling.DispersionGeneral
import RevPebbling.DispersionBound4731

/-!
# The general theorem subsumes the hardest instance (this work)

`DispersionGeneral.dispersion_general` takes the constants as parameters.
This file checks it against the sharpest hand-written instance,
`DispersionBound4731` (92 KB of digits, `q = 47/31`): the general theorem
plus that file's own two threshold `decide`s and three arithmetic ones
reproduce its main bound exactly.

So a new exponent no longer needs a new file — only new constants and
five decidable integer facts.  That is what makes the supremum over `q`
reachable: `c ≥ 2^{1+V(q)}` at every rational `q`, and
`κ* = sup_q V(q)`.
-/

namespace RevPebbling.DispersionRecover

open RevPebbling.DispersionGeneral RevPebbling.CountRecursion
open RevPebbling.DispersionBound4731 (threshold4731_branch1 threshold4731_branch2)

/-- **THE `q = 47/31` BOUND, FROM THE GENERAL THEOREM.**  Compare
`DispersionBound4731.dispersion4731_main`, which proves the same
statement with the whole induction written out. -/
theorem dispersion4731_from_general : ∀ s v, (:Nat) ^ s * countLe (rowIter s) v
    ≤ Wg 31 47 100000000 v *  ^ s :=
  dispersion_general 31 47 100000000     2894171759 3632550103 6526721862
    (by omega) (by omega) (by decide) (by omega)
    (by decide) (by omega) (by omega)
    (by decide) (by decide)
    threshold4731_branch1 threshold4731_branch2

end RevPebbling.DispersionRecover
